    "cards": [
        {
            "dot": "cyan",
            "title": "Client and local waypoint flow",
            "items": [
                "mods supplies both client and server adapters; paper supplies server adapters only. Vanilla clients can use remote commands and server switches without a mod.",
                "Local GUI, rendering, upload/download and map integration use local waypoint state. RemoteClientCatalogs is separate session-only state and never writes local waypoint caches or map mods.",
                "Protocol 11 carries chunked client/backend messages, including authorized remote catalog request/replacement messages."
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "rose",
            "title": "Cross-server control and ownership",
            "items": [
                "Each dedicated backend exports PUBLIC catalogs and holds a separate remote replica. Integrated servers do not start BackendRuntime.",
                "Velocity hosts catalog aggregation and the handoff coordinator. A second backend has the same backend structure shown here. TCP is bidirectional; arrows identify logical relationships, not one-way transport.",
                "Prepare destination, confirm source readiness, switch through Velocity, then claim and teleport on the destination owner. A server switch alone is not teleport completion.",
                "Mod callbacks run on the owning Minecraft server; Paper/Folia player callbacks use the player/entity owner. Lifecycle workers own blocking startup and transport cleanup."
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "amber",
            "title": "Trust, packaging and documentation",
            "items": [
                "Cross-server configuration is disabled by default. NOISE_KK uses pinned keys; explicit PLAINTEXT is unauthenticated and literal-loopback-only, with no downgrade fallback. Reserved plugin messages do not authorize handoffs.",
                "mods and paper depend on common. proxy-common depends on common; velocity depends on proxy-common and packages both libraries. Shared libraries are code, not standalone servers.",
                "Stonecutter selects backend loader/version projects. Velocity builds separately with Java 17 source syntax and a Java 25 runtime target. Final-artifact verification covers backend and Velocity release JARs.",
                "Features: docs/features/. Shared implementation guidance: docs/tips/. This overview: docs/architecture/. See each feature validation record for runtime evidence and release limitations."
            ]
        }
    ]
